"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7290],{17290:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"schemaVersion":1,"locale":"en","slug":"10-pervyj-portfel","title":"Lesson 10. First Portfolio: Assembly, Proportions, and Rebalancing","sourceFingerprint":"16f5d702af5d68a776e4d5677d3a9127d6f8c9c2e06077e4d64a4e7cf355abd6","blocks":[{"id":"b001","markdown":"### Section: Practical Asset Allocation \xb7 Chapter 10\\n*Reading time: 5 minutes*"},{"id":"b002","markdown":"# Architecture of the First Portfolio: Step-by-Step Construction for a Personal Horizon"},{"id":"b003","markdown":"> \\"The ideal investment portfolio is not created to impress the imagination with complexity or to guess tomorrow\'s quotes. Its sole task is to calmly and monotonically deliver you to financial independence on schedule.\\""},{"id":"b004","markdown":"You have done tremendous theoretical work: studied asset behavior over a twenty-year distance, determined the limit of personal psychological resilience to drawdowns, and analyzed the structure of reliable debt securities, currency diversification, and tax nuances."},{"id":"b005","markdown":"Now it is time to package abstract knowledge into a working applied model. To start, you will not need dozens of complex instruments or hundreds of individual stocks: an effective and stress-resilient structure can be launched using just **three to four basic funds** within a single properly configured account."},{"id":"b006","markdown":"## \uD83D\uDCCC Capital Construction Algorithm in 6 Sequential Steps"},{"id":"b007","markdown":"```\\n[1. Horizon and Goal] ➔ [2. Risk Proportion] ➔ [3. Fund Selection] ➔ [4. Tax Wrapper] ➔ [5. Auto-replenishment] ➔ [6. Annual Reconciliation]\\n```"},{"id":"b008","markdown":"### Step 1. Precise Quantification of the Horizon"},{"id":"b009","markdown":"There are no universal portfolios—each is assembled strictly for a specific objective. If you have multiple financial goals, a separate allocation is formed for each of them, even if the assets are formally held with a single broker:"},{"id":"b010","markdown":"1. **Core objective:** purchasing real estate, children\'s education, or passive income.\\n2. **Cost:** the amount of capital, fixed in today\'s purchasing power.\\n3. **Time horizon:** the exact year and month when funds will be withdrawn from the exchange."},{"id":"b011","markdown":"### Step 2. Determining the Boundary of Volatile Instruments"},{"id":"b012","markdown":"The time remaining until the event strictly dictates the **ceiling of the risk allocation**. The less time you have, the less right your capital has to a temporary drawdown."},{"id":"b013","markdown":"Within the acceptable range, be guided by your personal loss tolerance test: if an account drop of even 10% costs you sleep, stick to the lower boundary."},{"id":"b014","markdown":"#### Asset Allocation Matrix by Time Horizons"},{"id":"b015","markdown":"| Time interval until withdrawal | Share of volatile assets (stocks and gold) | Defensive perimeter (OFZ, deposits, money market) |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Under 3 years** | **0%** | **100%** |\\n| **From 3 to 7 years** | **no more than 30%** | **no less than 70%** |\\n| **From 7 to 15 years** | **no more than 60%** | **no less than 40%** |\\n| **Over 15 years** | **no more than 80%** | **no less than 20%** |"},{"id":"b016","markdown":"> **Take note:** you can always increase your equity allocation during calm times, but emotionally enduring an asset sell-off at the very bottom of the market is nearly impossible without years of experience."},{"id":"b017","markdown":"### Step 3. Filling the Basket with Instruments"},{"id":"b018","markdown":"Do not complicate the foundation by cherry-picking individual issuers. A single position is sufficient for each segment:"},{"id":"b019","markdown":"* **Equity market:** a broad market fund on the MOEX Index (includes dozens of systemically important corporations of the RF).\\n* **Fixed income:** a fund of classic OFZ with a constant coupon or specific government bond issues matched directly to your target date.\\n* **Crisis buffer:** an exchange-traded physical gold fund (#PAXG or gold on the MOEX) in an amount of 5–10% of the portfolio to hedge against ruble devaluation.\\n* **Instant liquidity:** a money market exchange-traded fund (LQDT) for holding reserves and regular top-ups."},{"id":"b020","markdown":"When selecting instruments, analyze three parameters: **the structure of the underlying index**, **total management fees**, and **instrument liquidity**."},{"id":"b021","markdown":"### Step 4. Choosing the Account Type and Tax Strategy"},{"id":"b022","markdown":"* **Horizon over 5 years:** open an **IIS-3**. This gives you the right to both an annual personal income tax refund on contributions (up to 52 000 – 88 000 ₽ for deposits up to 400 000 ₽) and a complete tax exemption on all earned investment income upon closing the account.\\n* **Horizon from 3 to 5 years:** use a standard brokerage account with an eye toward the long-term holding benefit (LDV), which exempts assets held for more than three years from capital gains tax.\\n* **Horizon under 3 years:** the stock market carries the risk of drawdowns. Keep money in high-yield savings accounts, term bank deposits, or money market instruments.\\n* **Income up to 80 000 ₽ per month:** simultaneously join the **Long-Term Savings Program (PDS)** to receive direct state co-financing of 1:1 (up to 36 000 ₽ annually)."},{"id":"b023","markdown":"### Step 5. Automating the Investment Process"},{"id":"b024","markdown":"Make the process independent of your willpower. Set up an automatic bank transfer to your brokerage account on your payday. Once a month, purchase the planned shares of funds manually or set up regular auto-investing with your broker."},{"id":"b025","markdown":"### Step 6. Annual Calibration Day (Once Every 365 Days)"},{"id":"b026","markdown":"Set a fixed date (for example, the New Year holidays or your birthday) for a mandatory check-up:"},{"id":"b027","markdown":"1. **Rebalancing:** compare current asset weights with target ones and restore the planned balance.\\n2. **Goals audit:** adjust parameters if your family income or the budget for a planned purchase has changed.\\n3. **Tax return:** submit an application to the Federal Tax Service for an investment deduction under IIS-3 for the completed calendar year."},{"id":"b028","markdown":"For all the remaining days of the year, the portfolio should grow without your intervention."},{"id":"b029","markdown":"## \uD83D\uDDC2 Portfolio Modeling Examples"},{"id":"b030","markdown":"```\\n[Marina, age 26]        [Artyom, age 34]          [Svetlana, age 51]\\nHorizon: 2.5 years      Horizon: 20 years         Horizon: 9 years\\nStocks: 0%              Stocks: 60%               Stocks: 30%\\nOFZ/Deposits: 100%      Bonds: 30%                Bonds: 50%\\nInstrument: Deposit     Gold: 10%                 Money market/Gold: 20%\\n                        Instrument: IIS-3         Instrument: Brokerage/IIS\\n```"},{"id":"b031","markdown":"### 1. Marina, 26 years old. Down payment for a mortgage in 2.5–3 years\\n* **Timeframe:** ultra-short. Volatility is unacceptable.\\n* **Structure:** 0% stocks. 50% of funds in an annual top-up deposit, 30% in an OFZ issue maturing exactly by the apartment purchase date, 20% of monthly savings directed to a money market fund.\\n* **Account:** regular brokerage account and deposits. IIS-3 is not used due to the inability to withdraw funds before 5 years without losing tax benefits."},{"id":"b032","markdown":"### 2. Artyom, 34 years old. Self-employed IT specialist. Capital for 20 years ahead\\n* **Timeframe:** ultra-long. High risk tolerance.\\n* **Structure:** 60% — MOEX index fund, 30% — sovereign OFZ, 10% — exchange-traded gold.\\n* **Account:** IIS-3. Since Artyom applies the professional income tax regime (NPD) and does not pay the standard 13% personal income tax, the contribution deduction is unavailable to him. However, IIS-3 exempts all future investment income from capital gains tax after 5 years, which will save hundreds of thousands of rubles. Since pension points do not accumulate automatically for self-employed individuals, Artyom maintains an emergency fund equal to 6 months of expenses."},{"id":"b033","markdown":"### 3. Svetlana, 51 years old. Preparing for retirement in 9 years\\n* **Timeframe:** moderate. It is critically important not to lose accumulated wealth before retirement.\\n* **Structure:** 30% — stock fund, 50% — OFZ (including OFZ-IN inflation-linked bonds with par value indexation), 10% — gold, 10% — money market fund. As she approaches age 60, the share of stocks is systematically reduced in favor of defensive assets."},{"id":"b034","markdown":"## \uD83D\uDEE1 Behavior During a Market Storm"},{"id":"b035","markdown":"When a panic sell-off begins in the market, an investor with an established architecture avoids chaotic actions:"},{"id":"b036","markdown":"* A personal emergency fund is kept separately in cash and deposits — there is no need to urgently sell fallen securities.\\n* The allocation to stocks is initially tailored to your nervous system — a drawdown does not trigger panic.\\n* Automatic deposits continue on schedule: the same amounts purchase significantly more depreciated assets.\\n* Annual rebalancing will automatically direct available cash to purchase temporarily beaten-down instruments."},{"id":"b037","markdown":"## \uD83E\uDDEE Notebook Margin Calculation: Tax-Free Rebalancing via Contributions"},{"id":"b038","markdown":"Imagine Artyom\'s portfolio of **1 000 000 ₽** with a baseline formula of **60% (stocks) / 30% (bonds) / 10% (gold)**."},{"id":"b039","markdown":"After a year of rapid market growth, the allocation shifted:\\n* **Stocks:** rose to 750 000 ₽ (began to account for **68%** instead of 60%).\\n* **Bonds:** fell to 260 000 ₽ (began to account for **24%** instead of 30%).\\n* **Gold:** stands at 90 000 ₽ (about **8%** instead of 10%).\\n* **Total value:** 1 100 000 ₽."},{"id":"b040","markdown":"```\\nCurrent imbalance:\\n[ Stocks: 68% (TARGET: 60%) ] ▲ OVERWEIGHT\\n[ Bonds: 24% (TARGET: 30%) ] ▼ DEFICIT\\n[ Gold: 8% (TARGET: 10%) ] ▼ DEFICIT\\n```"},{"id":"b041","markdown":"**Solution without selling assets:**  \\nTo avoid paying taxes and brokerage commissions for selling stocks, the balance is restored through the allocation of **fresh regular contributions**:"},{"id":"b042","markdown":"1. Artyom contributes **100 000 ₽** over the next 2–3 months. The total portfolio value becomes **1 200 000 ₽**.\\n2. Target benchmarks in monetary terms:\\n   * Stocks (60% of 1.2 million): **720 000 ₽** (already has 750 000 ₽ — purchases are temporarily frozen).\\n   * Bonds (30% of 1.2 million): **360 000 ₽** (need to buy an additional **100 000 ₽**).\\n   * Gold (10% of 1.2 million): **120 000 ₽** (need to buy an additional **30 000 ₽**).\\n3. All new deposits are directed exclusively into cheaper bonds and gold. Capital automatically returns to balance without stress and tax losses."}],"terms":[{"id":"asset-allocation","term":"Asset allocation","definition":"portfolio structure by asset class: stocks, bonds, cash, and gold."},{"id":"gorizont-investirovaniya","term":"Investment horizon","definition":"the period for which money is invested without plans to withdraw it."},{"id":"indeksnyj-bpif","term":"Equity ETF / BPIF","definition":"an exchange-traded fund tracking the structure of a market index."}],"quizzes":[{"id":"q1","q":"### 1. A family is saving for a mortgage down payment, with an apartment purchase planned in 2 years. What portion of the portfolio is permissible to allocate to equity ETFs?","options":["50%, to balance return and risk.","0%, for a period of up to 3 years capital is placed exclusively in defensive fixed-income instruments.","80%, to outpace real estate price growth as quickly as possible."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Over a short timeframe, the stock market can enter a prolonged drawdown, causing the family to lose a significant portion of their down payment by the time of purchase. Stocks are completely excluded for horizons under 3 years."},{"id":"q2","q":"### 2. An investor is registered as self-employed and pays the professional income tax. Why is it advantageous for them to use an IIS-3 account if they cannot claim the 13% personal income tax deduction on contributions?","options":["IIS-3 grants the right to receive guaranteed government payouts regardless of tax payments.","IIS-3 allows full exemption of all accumulated investment profit from taxes upon closing the account after the statutory period (from 5 years).","Brokers automatically double the dividend amount for self-employed individuals under government programs."],"correct":1,"explanation":"Even without paying standard personal income tax, an investor using IIS-3 retains the right to the second type of tax benefit — full exemption of capital gains from taxation upon closing the account after the minimum holding period."},{"id":"q3","q":"### 3. A severe crisis hit the market: stock prices plummeted by 35% in a couple of weeks. Which investor action aligns with the systematic MakeCash strategy?","options":["Urgently sell all equity funds to save remaining money and wait until the crisis is completely over.","Pause investments and close the brokerage account.","Keep calm, continue scheduled contributions, and purchase dipped assets during regular rebalancing according to pre-approved target allocations."],"correct":1,"explanation":"A market drop is a normal phase of a long-term cycle. A pre-allocated defensive portion and a separate emergency fund allow an investor to cool-headedly buy discounted assets at lower prices."}],"officialSources":[]}')}}]);